# Assistant Channel W1-D: the OAuth authorization server. Issue 2799. THE CRITICAL PATH.
#
# DISPATCH: ./scripts/dispatch.sh git@github.com:Arlencho/olympus-platform.git wave-plans/assistant-channel/2026-09-11-w1d-authserver.plan --auto --retries 2
#
# FIRE AFTER W1-B MERGES, because it mints the scope W1-B defines. No copy in
# this wave beyond a minimal test-only consent page; the real consent screen is
# W1-C on the web side. Three waves: producer, backend critic, security review.
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

1 | go-backend | Build the authorization server that lets a third-party assistant obtain a scoped Olympus token, and nothing else. READ FIRST: issue 2799 in full; issue 2797 for the verified facts; docs/prd/pages/assistant-channel.md for the identity decision and the token rules; apps/api/internal/handler/oauth_exchange.go, which is our own web sign-in and NOT this, so you understand what already exists and why it does not qualify; apps/api/internal/service/concierge.go lines 40 to 70 for the fifteen-minute user token pattern you will reuse; the concierge connect-token mint and redeem handlers; apps/api/internal/middleware/auth.go on main, including the assistant scope constant W1-B added; the W1-A migration for the assistant channel value; and the current authorization section of the protocol specification, which you may fetch with curl from the modelcontextprotocol dot io specification site, because it defines what a conforming host will do and you must implement to it rather than to memory. THE FACT THAT SHAPES THIS: a token with an empty scope is never scope-gated, auth.go line 128. Every token this server issues MUST carry the assistant scope, or the assistant reaches everything. WHAT TO BUILD, in apps/api. One, discovery: protected resource metadata and authorization server metadata endpoints at the well-known paths the specification names, advertising PKCE required and the assistant scope. Two, client registration for the two pilot hosts. Prefer static registration with per-host client identifiers and redirect allowlists over dynamic registration unless the specification makes dynamic mandatory for conforming hosts; state which and why in the PR body. Three, the authorize endpoint: requires PKCE with S256, validates client and redirect, and hands off to a login-or-signup step that reuses the existing magic link and Google sign-in handlers rather than reimplementing them. On consent it creates a channel binding with channel equal to assistant for that user and that client, which is what W1-B liveness keys on. Signup inside this flow goes through the existing invite gate unchanged; a not-invited email must get the existing not_invited refusal, and the pilot testers are pre-invited by decision on 2799. Serve a minimal server-rendered consent page for this wave with every string marked PROPOSED and taken from the W0 spec where one exists; the real screen is W1-C. Four, the token endpoint: exchanges the code for a short-lived access token bound to the assistant scope and to an audience naming this channel, following the fifteen-minute pattern, plus a rotating refresh token; rejects a missing or wrong code verifier; rejects a client mismatch. Five, revocation: revoking deactivates the binding, which per W1-B kills the access token at the next request, and invalidates the refresh token. Six, NO PASSTHROUGH: the API must never accept a host-issued token as an Olympus credential. Add an explicit test that presenting a foreign bearer token to a scoped route is 401. TESTS ARE THE POINT, and each must be proven to FAIL on the parent commit in a throwaway worktree with real exit codes captured directly rather than read after a pipe: PKCE required, S256 only; audience enforced, a token minted for any other audience is 403 on assistant routes; every issued token carries the assistant scope, asserted by decoding it; consent creates exactly one active assistant binding; revoke kills the next request and the refresh; refresh rotation invalidates the old refresh token; a not-invited signup inside the flow is refused before any row; a foreign bearer is 401; redirect to an unregistered URI is refused. Run go build, go vet, the api unit suite with race, golangci-lint and make check-api-spec, and paste real exit codes. Update api.yaml for every new route and run make generate; the bidirectional route gate will fail otherwise. Commit before you break anything; never git stash; never git checkout to revert. Open a PR referencing issue 2799 with Refs and no closing keyword. HOUSE STYLE: no long dash anywhere, no AI tool or vendor name in commit or PR text, no Co-Authored-By trailer. Do not merge. | feat/ac-w1d-authserver

2 | backend-critic | READ-ONLY REVIEW of the wave 1 change on branch feat/ac-w1d-authserver. You are not a producer: no commits, no push, no gh pr review, no gh pr merge, no labels, no test writing. The harness pushes the branch it checks out, so any commit on a producer ref is a production incident. Permitted writes are a slash tmp worktree only. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report; if it moved report BLOCKED with both SHAs. THE QUESTION THAT MATTERS: can this server ever hand out a token that reaches more than the assistant allowlist. Drive it, do not read it. FIRST, run the full authorize-consent-token flow yourself against the real router in a throwaway worktree and decode every token it issues. Any token without the assistant scope claim is a BLOCK, because auth.go line 128 means an unscoped token is never gated. Any token whose audience is missing or generic is a BLOCK. SECOND, attempt the bypasses: token request without a code verifier, with a wrong verifier, with a plain rather than S256 challenge, with a mismatched client, with an unregistered redirect, and with a host-issued bearer presented directly to a scoped route. Each must be refused; state the status for each. THIRD, revoke a binding and confirm the access token is refused on the very next request and the refresh token is dead, then confirm a concierge token for the same user is untouched. FOURTH, signup inside the flow: a pre-invited email creates an account and a binding; a not-invited email is refused before any row, reproduced by checking the users table. FIFTH, confirm the existing web sign-in at oauth_exchange.go is unchanged and its tests still pass, since reusing the magic link and Google handlers must not have altered them. SIXTH, confirm api.yaml covers every new route and make check-api-spec exits 0. SEVENTH, confirm the fail-first claims by checking out the parent and running the new tests yourself. EIGHTH, no long dash, no vendor name, no trailer. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads CRITIC ASSISTANT CHANNEL W1D AUTHSERVER, with a verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE and per-finding file and line citations. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | feat/ac-w1d-authserver

3 | security-reviewer | SECURITY REVIEW of branch feat/ac-w1d-authserver, an OAuth authorization server that issues scoped tokens to third-party assistants. Read-only: no commits, no push, no review state, no labels. Record the PR head SHA at start and re-check at report. Review as an attacker who controls a malicious assistant client and wants either a token for another user, a token with more reach than the assistant scope, or a way to keep access after the user disconnects. Cover at minimum: authorization code interception and reuse, PKCE downgrade, redirect manipulation including open redirect on the login hop, client impersonation, token substitution across audiences, refresh token theft and rotation gaps, the binding lifecycle race between consent and revoke, whether the well-known metadata leaks anything it should not, whether error responses leak whether an email exists given the invite gate, and whether any log line in the new code writes a token, a code or a verifier. Confirm the no-passthrough property holds at the middleware, not only at the endpoint. Post ONE comment on issue 2340 whose first line reads SECURITY ASSISTANT CHANNEL W1D, with severity per finding and file and line citations, and an overall verdict of SAFE-TO-MERGE or BLOCK-FIX or BLOCK-ESCALATE. No vendor names, emails, booking ids or gcloud payloads. | feat/ac-w1d-authserver
